# of them was concurrency.
# Source: runs 37059411089 .. 37074944913 on main, https://github.com/chao2hang/chaos-code
# Fetched with: gh run list / gh api repos/chao2hang/chaos-code/actions/runs/<id>/jobs
# Host that fetched it: Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64, gh 2.x. No Windows or macOS machine ran here.

== 1. Eight heads, three real runs, five thrown away before a runner

    $ gh run list --branch main --limit 8 \
        --json databaseId,headSha,status,conclusion \
        --jq '.[] | "\(.databaseId) \(.headSha[0:8]) \(.status)/\(.conclusion)"'
    37074944913 f585f59d completed/cancelled
    37072608076 4db25faf completed/cancelled
    37071514793 ef33dfd9 completed/cancelled
    37066345380 1487aded completed/failure
    37064187465 950ce77d completed/cancelled
    37061975515 22683f8c completed/failure
    37060343678 0f1b8818 completed/cancelled
    37059411089 a6984491 completed/cancelled

Five of the eight never started a job. The count is taken from the API rather than from
the conclusion string, because `cancelled` alone does not distinguish "queued and then
thrown away" from "ran and was stopped":

    $ for r in 37072608076 37071514793 37064187465 37060343678 37059411089; do
        printf "%s jobs=" $r
        gh api repos/chao2hang/chaos-code/actions/runs/$r/jobs --jq '.jobs | length'
      done
    37072608076 jobs=0
    37071514793 jobs=0
    37064187465 jobs=0
    37060343678 jobs=0
    37059411089 jobs=0

Zero jobs means the run never got a runner. That is the `concurrency` group doing what it
is configured to do -- `cancel-in-progress: false` only protects a run that is *already
running*; a newer push still discards runs sitting in the queue for the same ref:

    concurrency:
      group: ci-${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}
      cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}

Nothing here is a code defect, and `timeout-minutes` or `needs` would not change it. The
cost is real though: `rust` takes ~42 min on the runner (see the timings below), the
platform legs hang off it, and three commits pushed inside one hour produced no platform
evidence at all. The corrective is procedural -- push a batch of finished slices rather
than one push per slice -- and it is why the section after this one is appended before
the push that carries it.

== 2. The newest head was not a concurrency casualty: the Windows leg hit its own cap

