          l10n-guard: rg (ripgrep) not found
    FAIL  clobber_in_place
          clobber: exit 64, want 1
          l10n-guard: rg (ripgrep) not found
    (17 cases, same three lines each)

No translation file was broken. The guard needs `rg`, that runner has no ripgrep, and the
guard refuses to conclude without it -- `exit 64` is the fail-closed behaviour added in the
previous round, so this red was the guard working. What was missing was an `apt-get install
ripgrep` step in the job that runs it, and nothing in `ci.yml` paired "tools a step needs"
with "tools the job installs"; that gap is now a guard of its own. Root cause, fix and the
new guard: [`workflow-toolchain-2026-10-03.log`](workflow-toolchain-2026-10-03.log).

Consequence for platform evidence, stated precisely because it is easy to get wrong:
`platform tests` has `needs: rust` and no dependency on `docs localization`, so this red
never cancelled the platform legs. What it did do was make `main` carry a permanent red,
which turns "did this round's CI actually run?" into an unanswerable question -- and an
unanswerable question is what let the Windows cap hide for five heads.

== 4. What this section is still not evidence about

No Windows or macOS machine was used to produce anything above; every number comes from the
Actions API for runs other machines executed. The Windows test suite has still never
reported a result of its own, so nothing here says the six target-OS crates pass on
Windows -- only that the leg is now given a budget it can plausibly finish inside, and that
the two causes of silence (queued-run cancellation, per-job timeout) are named separately
instead of being filed together as "CI is flaky".

== 5. The Windows leg finally reached the tests, and 16m43s of that leg was one string

Run `37074944913` / job `111075816970` (`f585f59d`) is the first Windows run that got as far
as executing tests. Section 2 read it as "spent its entire budget inside the test step" and
stopped there because the step-level breakdown was all that was available then. The step log
has since been fetched, and it does not say "slow"; it says one test never returned.

The step's own timeline, from the job log:

    23:48:37            step 9 starts: cargo test --locked --no-fail-fast
    00:14:20.97         first `test result:` line (compile took 25m43s)
    00:14:21 - 00:14:24 five other test binaries finish, within ~4 s of each other
    00:14:24.04         xai_grok_tools-c0c13642df1bb12b.exe starts: `running 3087 tests`
    00:15:45.28         test ...cwd_and_worktree_isolation_are_mutually_exclusive has been
                        running for over 60 seconds
    00:15:59.27         last verdict printed by any thread
    00:32:42.02         ##[error]The operation was canceled.

    # verdict lines inside the xai_grok_tools test binary only (the 3087-test block)
    grep -c '\.\.\. ok'          -> 2960
    grep -c '\.\.\. FAILED'      -> 124
    grep -c '\.\.\. ignored'     -> 2
                                    # +1 that never returned = 3087

2960 + 124 + 2 = 3086 of the 3087 launched. The missing one is named verbatim in the log:

    2026-10-03T00:15:45.2830227Z test implementations::grok_build::task::tests::cwd_and_worktree_isolation_are_mutually_exclusive has been running for over 60 seconds

After that line, 16m43s of nothing, then the job cap. libtest prints each verdict as its
thread finishes and prints the `test result:` summary only once every thread has returned. A
binary with one stuck test therefore prints no summary -- and no `failures:` section, which
is where the assertion text lives. That is why the 124 failures below have names but no
messages: the messages were never written. It is inferred from libtest's printing order, not
quoted from a source I can point at; what is quoted is the absence -- 124 `FAILED` verdicts
and zero `panicked at` lines for the tools binary.

== 5a. The hang: one fixture string selected a different branch on Windows

`TaskTool::run` validates `cwd` + `isolation="worktree"` mutual exclusion through a single
existence check (`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grok_build/task/mod.rs`,
the branch at line 468):

    let cwd = if cwd.is_some() && input.isolation == Some(SubagentIsolationMode::Worktree) {
        if cwd.as_deref().is_some_and(|p| std::path::Path::new(p).is_dir()) {
            return Err(ToolError::invalid_arguments("cwd and isolation=\"worktree\" ..."));
        }
        None                     // non-existent path: clear cwd, worktree wins
    } else { cwd };

The test passed `cwd: Some("/tmp".into())` meaning "an existing directory". On Linux and
macOS `/tmp` exists, the branch returns `Err`, the test asserts on the error and finishes in
milliseconds. On `windows-latest` there is no `C:\tmp`, `is_dir()` is false, the `cwd` is
cleared, and the tool goes on to spawn a subagent -- into a backend whose response receiver
the test never reads, because a test that expects validation to reject the input has nothing
to read. `Tool::run` then awaits a response that is never sent. That is a hang, not a
failure, and it is indistinguishable from a slow machine from outside the box.

Proof that the test is not vacuous was run on Linux by mutating the branch, not the test.
Replacing the condition with `is_some_and(|_p| false)` -- i.e. making the production code
take exactly the path Windows took -- turns the test red in precisely the way the theory
predicts:

    running 1 test
    test implementations::grok_build::task::tests::cwd_and_worktree_isolation_are_mutually_exclusive ... FAILED
    ...
    thread '...cwd_and_worktree_isolation_are_mutually_exclusive' panicked at
      crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grok_build/task/mod.rs:2423:10:
    cwd + worktree must be rejected by validation, not wait on a subagent: Elapsed(())

    test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 3163 filtered out; finished in 30.00s

Reverting the mutation restores `test result: ok. 1 passed; ... finished in 0.00s`, and the
restored file was compared byte-for-byte against a pre-mutation copy (`cmp` reports no
difference). The 30-second ceiling is new, not incidental: see the fix below.

== 5b. The same string, three tests sideways, and the rest of the fixture family

Three sibling tests in the same module failed for the same reason and did not hang, because
they assert on the spawned request rather than on a rejection -- on Windows the `cwd` they
expected had been silently cleared:

    implementations::grok_build::task::tests::cwd_strips_stray_leading_quote ... FAILED
    implementations::grok_build::task::tests::cwd_threads_to_request ... FAILED
    implementations::grok_build::task::tests::cwd_with_isolation_none_is_allowed ... FAILED

The tests that passed on Windows in the same module are the evidence for the mechanism: the
ones using `/nonexistent/...` or a `/tmp/some-dir` + `resume_from` combination never touch
`is_dir()` meaningfully, so they behave identically on both hosts. Existence-dependence of
the fixture, not the choice of `/tmp` as a name, is what made those tests host-specific.

The fix is to stop writing "an existing directory" as a literal. In `xai-grok-tools`, a
test-module helper `existing_dir()` returns `std::env::temp_dir().display().to_string()` and
the five `cwd:` fixtures use it; the two validation tests additionally wrap the call in
`tokio::time::timeout(VALIDATION_TIMEOUT, ..)` (30 s) so that taking the spawn branch by
accident fails with a named message instead of eating the job budget. The same fixture
family was replaced in eight more places that only display or thread the path through --
`xai-grok-shell-terminal/src/adapter_tests.rs`, `background_task.rs` (two sites),
`xai-grok-tools/src/computer/types.rs` (two), `implementations/task_output/tool.rs`,
`implementations/grok_build/task_output/mod.rs` -- plus the 54-failure block in
`computer/local/terminal.rs` (14 sites, behind `shell_path()` / `pwd_reports_dir()` helpers
so the assertion still checks the real `pwd` output rather than a path string).

The other two Windows failures with readable text, both fixed in the same pass:

    streaming_local_terminal::tests::test_kill_returns_signal
      panicked at crates\codegen\xai-grok-shell-terminal\src\streaming_local_terminal.rs:1234:17
      left: None, right: Some("signal 9")      -- Windows kills do not report a signal
    update_never_blocked_by_config::corrupt_config_never_changes_update_outcome
      os error 10106                           -- Winsock unavailable: the test stripped the
                                                 environment the updater needs to init it

The 124 failures are grouped below by module, because that is the level the log supports.
Their assertion text does not exist anywhere in this run, so no cause is claimed for any
group beyond the three lines above whose text was actually printed:

    54  computer::local::terminal::tests                    -- host-pinned /tmp, fixed above
    38  implementations::lsp::*                             -- text unknown, read next run
     7  types::skill_discovery_tracker::skill_path_suggestion::tests
     5  types::resources::tests                             -- resolve_model_path_*
     5  implementations::grok_build::read_file::tests
     3  implementations::grok_build::task::tests            -- fixed above
     3  implementations::grok_build::bash::tests
