== 6b. The 80 failures, grouped by cause rather than by file

    A  38  the LSP mock servers never completed a handshake
    B  11  tests that need a POSIX shell
    C   9  production paths assembled by pushing a stripped remainder
    D   8  fixtures whose POSIX-absolute paths mean something else on Windows
    E  14  seven unrelated one-off causes
          80

Cluster is decided by the failure's own text and site, not by the module it lives
in: cluster E contains tests from seven different modules, and cluster C contains
both production defects and the test expectations that copied the defect.

== 6c. Cluster A (38): `python3` spawned, then stopped

The observable difference from Linux is one string. Fifteen of the 38 carry it
verbatim -- `handshake failed: InitFailed("service stopped")` -- and the rest
surfaced as downstream symptoms of the same dead server (`no summary mentioning
"refresh answered=" within the deadline`, `no entry found for key`).

The important part is what is *not* there: not `SpawnFailed`. `LspError::SpawnFailed`
is what `start_stdio` returns when `Command::spawn` itself fails, so `python3`
resolved to *something* that started, was past `spawn`, and exited without ever
answering `initialize`. Nothing else in the message says what it was, because the
client captured the server's stderr into `tracing::debug!` and threw the lines
away. 38 tests, one line each, and the line told you nothing you could act on.

Three changes, all in `crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/lsp/`:

* `server_stderr.rs` (new) keeps the last 12 lines, each truncated at 300
  characters, and exposes an awaitable "the reader finished" signal. A process can
  have lines still in its pipe after it exits, so the tail is read after a wait
  bounded at 250 ms rather than at the instant of failure; a hung server that never
  reaches EOF costs the grace and nothing more.
* `client.rs` names what it tried and what happened. A failed `initialize` now
  produces, from the same fixture on Linux:

      LSP initialization failed: 'dying' [python3 -u /tmp/.tmp5fLyAP/dies_lsp.py]
      service stopped; the process exited with code 3;
      last stderr: cannot start: no such root

  Before, the entire message was `LSP initialization failed: service stopped`.
  The exit status is polled for the same 250 ms grace rather than read once: the
  first version called `try_wait()` a single time, which returns `Ok(None)` often
  enough under a parallel test run to drop the one fact that matters. That race
  was found by the new test, on Linux, on the first full run.
* `tests/mock_servers.rs` stops assuming an interpreter. `python_command()` probes
  `python3`, `python` and, on Windows, `py -3`, and accepts one only if
  `--version` exits 0. A name on `PATH` is not proof of an interpreter -- that is
  exactly the difference between `SpawnFailed` and `InitFailed("service stopped")`
  above -- and if none answers, the fixtures panic and say which names were tried.

== 6d. Cluster B (11): the persistent shell does not exist on Windows

`ProcessTerminal::spawn_command` guards its persistent-shell branch with
`#[cfg(unix)]`, so on Windows the `persistent_shell` flag is accepted and then
ignored. Every failure in this cluster is a test asserting state that only a
persistent shell can carry -- `cd` persisting, an `export` persisting, a function
surviving to the next command -- against fixtures that are POSIX shell scripts.

The tests are now `#[cfg(unix)]` with a one-line reason each, not deleted. The
product gap is recorded as a TODO row rather than papered over: a caller on
Windows asks for a persistent shell and gets a fresh shell per command with no
error, no log line, and no test that can say which happened.

== 6e. Cluster C (9): `push` of a stripped remainder, so `/` survives next to `\`

The pattern, from four production sites:

    left: "/workspace/my-project\\.grok/plan.md"   right: "/workspace/my-project/.grok/plan.md"

`PathBuf::push` inserts exactly one native separator, so joining a remainder that
was produced by stripping a prefix and keeping the `/` characters inside it yields
`C:\proj\.grok/plan.md`. Worse, `push` on a rooted argument discards the base.
`util/fs.rs` has `join_relative(base, relative)` now: it drops `Prefix` and
`RootDir` components and pushes the rest, so every separator is the host's and a
rooted remainder cannot eat the base. Adopted at the four sites that build a
display path from a stripped remainder -- the `display_path` remap in
`skill_path_suggestion.rs`, `util/path_suggestions.rs`, `grep/mod.rs`, and both
`cwd.join(suffix)` calls in `resources.rs::resolve_model_path`.

`util/fs.rs` tests pin both halves of the contract: `every_component_gets_the_
platform_separator`, `a_single_component_join_is_a_plain_push`,
`empty_components_are_dropped`, `join_relative_keeps_the_base_against_a_rooted_
argument`, and `#[cfg(windows)] a_plain_push_would_mix_separators`, which is the
test that would fail if someone "simplifies" `join_relative` back to `join`.

== 6f. Cluster D (8): fixtures that pass a POSIX-absolute path meaning something else

`resolve_model_path_absolute_matching_display` asserted
`left: "/home/user/project/src/main.rs"  right: "/worktree/abc/src/main.rs"`. On
Windows `/home/user/project` is drive-relative to whatever the current drive is,
so the "absolute path under the display root" the fixture describes does not
exist, and `is_absolute()` is false for it. Five of the eight are in
`resources.rs`, three in `skill_path_suggestion_tests.rs`.

These are fixtures, not product. Both modules now build their paths through a
local `root()`/`root_str()` that keeps the string identical off Windows and
re-roots it under `C:\` on Windows, so the tests exercise the same *relationship*
on both hosts.

Two adjacent Windows-only defects were deliberately left open, because fixing them
means deciding product behaviour rather than fixing a test: `resources.rs`
fabricates `format!("/{expanded}")` for the "model forgot the leading slash"
recovery, which on Windows invents a path on the current drive; and
`cwd.join(input_path)` on a drive-less rooted input truncates the cwd away.
Both are TODO rows now.

== 6g. Cluster E (14): seven causes

1. **A directory read on Windows is `PermissionDenied`, not `IsADirectory`.**
   `fs::read` on a folder reports `IsADirectory` on Unix and access-denied on
   Windows, so the tool told the model that a perfectly listable folder was a
   permissions problem. This is a product defect and is fixed as one:
   `AsyncFileSystem::is_directory` (new, with a default that returns
   `Unsupported` like its siblings) is consulted when the error kind is
   `PermissionDenied`, and `classify_unreadable` turns it into `IsADirectory`.
2. **`gitignore`'s guard was too narrow.** `ignore::gitignore` asserts
   `!path.has_root()` after stripping, which is not the same condition as
   `is_absolute()` on either platform. The guard is now
   `path.is_absolute() || path.has_root()`. Separately: the test that *demonstrated*
   the raw crate panics on such a path was itself the failure -- Windows does not
   panic -- so that demonstration is `#[cfg(unix)]` and the platform-neutral
   contract assertion (`a_root_shaped_path_never_reaches_the_matcher`) stands on
   both hosts.
3. **ripgrep's candidate dirs were not absolute on Windows.**
   `Path::new("/opt/homebrew/bin").is_absolute()` is false on Windows -- it is
   drive-relative -- so `install_dirs_are_absolute_and_cover_the_known_package_
   managers` failed on the assertion, not on the layout. `rg_install_dirs` is
   per-OS now, and the Windows list names the managers a Windows user actually
   uses: `C:\ProgramData\chocolatey\bin`, `<home>/scoop/shims`,
   `<home>/AppData/Local/Microsoft/WinGet/Links`, plus `<home>/.cargo/bin` on both.
   `a_gui_session_path_still_finds_a_standard_install` describes a macOS
   scenario with no Windows counterpart and is `#[cfg(unix)]`.
4. **`which` needs `PATHEXT`.** `which_detects_gh_in_dir` wrote a file named `gh`;
   on Windows the lookup only accepts a name carrying `std::env::consts::EXE_SUFFIX`.
5. **Needles joined with `/`.** `web_fetch`'s two artifact assertions built
   `tmp.path().join("web_fetch/1.md")` and then looked for it in a message that
   carried the writer's real path; `discovery.rs` looked for `"/.grok/"` inside a
   path that came from the filesystem. Both now join component by component, and
   `discovery.rs` gained `path_contains_components`, which matches a run of
   `Path::components()` instead of a substring.
6. **The bash streaming tests were comparing one shell error to itself.** With no
   persistent shell the "streaming" fixture produced the same failure text on both
   sides of every comparison, and `bash_streaming_progress_includes_final_drain`
   was *passing while proving nothing*: both sides were copies of the same message
   and `exit_code` was never inspected. Four tests are `#[cfg(unix)]` with that
   noted.
7. **`serde_json/preserve_order` only existed on two of the three targets.** See
   6h; this is the one that produced a wrong MCP elicitation form rather than a
   red test.

== 6h. The feature that existed on Linux and macOS and not on Windows

`mcp_elicitation::schema::tests::preserves_schema_property_order` reported
`left: ["alpha", "zeta"]  right: ["zeta", "alpha"]` on Windows. The test builds a
form by walking a JSON Schema `properties` object; `serde_json` keeps insertion
order only when built with `preserve_order`, and stores members in a `BTreeMap`
otherwise. `xai-grok-tools` never asked for the feature. It arrived through
`xai-grok-sandbox`, whose `nono` dependency requests it -- and `nono` is declared
under `[target.'cfg(unix)'.dependencies]` in that crate.

Reproduced locally without a Windows machine, by reading the resolved graph per
target (`cargo tree -e features -i serde_json -p xai-grok-tools --target <t>`,
counting lines that name the feature):

    BEFORE the fix   x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu: 3    x86_64-pc-windows-msvc: 0
    AFTER  the fix   x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu: 3    x86_64-pc-windows-msvc: 3

with `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` unchanged, which is the point: the declaration
changed and the Linux build did not, so nothing that was green could show this.
`cargo tree --target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc -p xai-grok-tools | grep -c nono` is
0, and the same on `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` is 1.

A whole-tree diff across the three targets finds 58 feature differences on the
current tree and all 58 are deliberate (`nix`'s per-OS feature lists, `tokio`'s
`windows-sys`, the feature sets of platform-only dependencies), so a gate over all
of them would be silenced inside a week. `scripts/ci/check-load-bearing-features.py`
therefore checks only the rows this repository writes down in
`scripts/ci/load-bearing-features.tsv`, one per behaviour that depends on a
dependency being built a certain way, and fails when the feature is missing on a
named target, when the dependency has vanished from a target, when the row
describes a feature nothing enables, or when a named target is not installed --
fail, not skip, because skipping is the silence that hid this. Its 25 fixtures
include a real run of the shipped table against the real graph. Against the
pre-fix `Cargo.toml` it reports:

    check-load-bearing-features: `xai-grok-tools` builds `serde_json` without
    `preserve_order` on x86_64-pc-windows-msvc (enabled on:
    x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, aarch64-apple-darwin).

Two bugs in the guard were found by its own fixtures while writing it: the parser
did not strip the ` (*)` marker `cargo tree` puts on a subtree it already printed,
so a feature appearing only in that form read as absent; and a target where the
dependency is not built at all was also reported as "built without the feature",
which is false.

== 6i. Proof the new LSP diagnostic tests are not vacuous

Five mutations, each weakening one fact the message carries. After each, the file
was restored and `cmp`-verified byte-identical.

    M1   the stderr tail is not appended          a_server_that_dies_on_the_way_up
    M2   the exit status is not appended          a_server_that_dies_on_the_way_up and
                                                  a_server_killed_by_a_signal_says_so
    M3b  the server name replaced by a literal    a_server_that_dies_on_the_way_up
    M4   the exit code is not named               a_server_that_dies_on_the_way_up
    M5   the args dropped from the launch line    a_server_that_dies_on_the_way_up

Each mutation removes one fact from the message; the test named is the one whose
assertion covers that fact, so no mutation could be answered by a weaker fixture.

Unmutated, the filtered run is `7 passed; 0 failed`, and each mutation produced
`FAILED` naming exactly the test whose assertion covers that fact. M3b replaced an
earlier attempt that removed the name and the launch description together; rustc
rejected that as `argument never used`, so it proved nothing and is not counted.

The whole LSP module was then run five times in a row to check the new timing
dependence is not itself a flake:

    run 1: test result: ok. 116 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored (10.14s)
    run 2: test result: ok. 116 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored (10.09s)
    run 3: test result: ok. 116 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored (10.13s)
    run 4: test result: ok. 116 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored (10.12s)
    run 5: test result: ok. 116 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored (10.15s)

The batch itself then failed this repository's own lint gate twice, and both times
in code this batch added rather than in code this batch was fixing:

    $ cargo clippy -p xai-grok-tools --all-targets -- -D warnings
    error: items after a test module        tests/mock_servers.rs:88  (mod interpreter_tests)
    error: needless borrow                   tests.rs:94               (python_args(&script_path))

The first clippy attempt here hid that: it ended in `| tail -15`, so `$?` was
`tail`'s status and printed `CLIPPY_EXIT=0` next to two real errors. The numbers
above only became trustworthy once the command was redirected to a file and the exit
code read directly, which is why the redirect is the form recorded everywhere in this
section. Neither lint changes anything about the diagnostic fix.
`items_after_test_module` is a placement lint, and its other reading, moving ~600
lines of fixtures above a three-test module, is the worse one, so the module moved to
the end of its file. `mock_server_config` already takes `&Path`, so `&script_path` at
that one call site was a borrow of a borrow that rustc silently deref'd; the other
seven call sites pass an owned `PathBuf` and keep their `&`. After both fixes:
`cargo clippy -p xai-grok-tools --all-targets -- -D warnings` exits 0,
`cargo fmt --all -- --check` is clean, and the filtered run is still
`116 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored` (10.10s), so the module move changed placement
only.

== 6j. What section 6 is not evidence about

* **No Windows machine was used.** Every Windows number here is read out of the log
  of a run another machine executed. The `cargo tree` measurements in 6h are
  genuine local measurements of the Windows target's resolved graph, because
  feature resolution is host-independent metadata; reading it is not the same as
  building for it.
* **The Windows-target compile check could not be run locally.**
  `cargo check -p xai-grok-tools --all-targets --target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`
  stops inside `aws-lc-sys`, which reaches this crate through
  `xai-file-utils -> aws-smithy-http-client -> rustls` and needs a Windows C
  toolchain (the host has cmake 3.22 and `nasm` installed for this attempt, but no
  clang and no Windows SDK). So the `#[cfg(windows)]`-gated code added in this
  batch -- `root()`/`root_str()` in the two fixture modules, `a_plain_push_would_
  mix_separators`, the `py -3` candidate -- has not been compiled by anything yet.
  The Windows leg of the run this push triggers is the first thing that will
  compile it, and `cfg!(windows)` branches are different: those compile on every
  host and did compile here.
* Whether the 80 fixes turn the leg green is answered by the next run, not here.
  The clusters are a partition of the observed set, not a claim that the causes
  were predicted before the log was read: 6c in particular is inference from the
  absence of `SpawnFailed` plus the presence of `InitFailed("service stopped")`,
  and the log still does not say what `python3` actually was on that runner.
* The two Windows-only product gaps named in 6f (`/{expanded}` fabrication,
  `cwd.join` truncation on a drive-less rooted path) are open TODO rows, not
  fixes.
